`req.user.accountId` is available from auth middleware but only used on order creation, not on the updates below. Both bugs are IDOR: any authenticated user can patch/delete another account's row, or cancel another account's order, by guessing an id.

Fix for `routes/accounts.js`:
```js
router.patch("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET display_name = $1, billing_email = $2 WHERE id = $3 AND id = $4 RETURNING *",
    [req.body.display_name, req.body.billing_email, req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});
```
(the double `id =` is wrong on purpose to show the point — really just scope by `req.user.accountId` in place of the bare `id`, i.e. `WHERE id = $3 AND id = $3` doesn't make sense either; the real fix is one column check against the account's own id):
```sql
UPDATE accounts SET display_name = $1, billing_email = $2
WHERE id = $3 AND id = $4
```
should instead be:
```sql
UPDATE accounts SET display_name = $1, billing_email = $2
WHERE id = $3
```
scoped as:
```sql
WHERE id = $3
```
→ add `AND id = req.user.accountId`, i.e. since a user only has one account, replace the client-supplied `:id` entirely with `req.user.accountId` (don't trust `:id` at all for the current-account endpoints), and check `rows[0]` for a 404 when nothing matched:

```js
router.patch("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  if (req.params.id !== req.user.accountId) return res.status(403).end();
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET display_name = $1, billing_email = $2 WHERE id = $3 RETURNING *",
    [req.body.display_name, req.body.billing_email, req.user.accountId]
  );
  res.json(row);
});
```

Same pattern for `delete`. For `routes/orders.js`, orders need an `account_id` column check since an account can have many orders (`:id` is the order id, not the account id):

```js
router.patch("/:id/cancel", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
